WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.200
Traditional antivirus focused heavily on identifying known malicious files,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.695
often using signatures.

00:00:05.695 --> 00:00:06.730
That is still useful.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.590
Known malware should be blocked when possible,

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.020
and prevention remains an important security layer.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.850
Attackers do not always rely on obvious malware files.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.820
They may use legitimate tools, scripts, stolen credentials,

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.755
or built-in operating system features.

00:00:18.755 --> 00:00:20.550
EDR looks more broadly at behavior.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.440
A strange PowerShell command, a process dumping credentials,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.040
an unusual parent-child process relationship,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.515
or a suspicious outbound connection may all matter.

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:30.260
EDR does not replace prevention.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.370
It complements prevention by helping defenders detect and investigate activity that gets

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:35.480
past earlier controls.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.970
The practical difference is that older antivirus often asks whether a file is known to be

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:40.120
bad,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.720
while EDR also asks what the endpoint is doing and whether that behavior makes sense.
